= White Tea (白茶)

_Same species as the other teas (Camellia sinensis); the differentiation is by minimal processing — withered and dried only, with no kill-green (殺青) pan-firing and no rolling, giving the least-manipulated of the six tea categories. "White" names the silvery-white down (白毫 báiháo) on the unopened buds, not the liquor colour. Like oolong, white tea as a named commercial category postdates the classical Bencao: the sun-withered Fujian grades (Silver Needle, White Peony) are a Qing-dynasty development, and the 白茶 of Song sources is a different referent (a rare pale-leaf tribute cultivar). Its classical-pharmacy status is therefore reasoned on the same processing → temperature axis as the rest of the family — white sitting at the coolest, least-processed end — anchored on the Chinese 茶 (chá) entry, the modern Fuding folk-medical tradition, and the tea framework set out in_ green-tea.typ. _Ayurveda and Tibb: not classical. Greco-Roman: not classical (tea unknown to antiquity)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Camellia sinensis_ (L.) Kuntze, family Theaceae — the same species as green / oolong / red / pu-erh tea. Processing, not botany, defines the category; see *Processing and category definition* below. The premium white teas use large-budded cultivars, notably *大白 (Dà Bái, "Big White")* and *大毫 (Dà Háo, "Big Down")*.

*English:* _White tea_ — a calque of Chinese *白茶 (báichá)*; "white" refers to the *silvery-white trichomes* (fine down) covering the unopened leaf-buds, most visible on Silver Needle, not to the pale straw colour of the infusion. A related term of trade, *pekoe*, descends from the same 白毫 (báiháo, "white down"): via Amoy/Hokkien *pe̍h-ho* it entered English tea-grading as "pekoe" (as in "orange pekoe"), naming the downy bud-tip leaf.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "white tea"; "pekoe, n."): "pekoe" < Amoy (Hokkien) _pe̍h-ho_, corresponding to Mandarin 白毫 _báiháo_ ("white down/hair"), naming the downy young leaf-bud. The "white" of white tea is the bud-down, not the liquor colour.]

*Chinese:* *白茶 (báichá)*, "white tea," one of the *六大茶類 (liù dà chá lèi,* six great tea categories); made chiefly in *福鼎 (Fúdǐng)* and *政和 (Zhènghé)* counties of Fujian. The classic grade hierarchy, by pluck:
- *白毫銀針 (Bái Háo Yín Zhēn, "White-Down Silver Needle")* — *buds only*; the highest grade, silvery, sweet, delicate.
- *白牡丹 (Bái Mǔdān, "White Peony")* — *one bud + one-to-two leaves*; fuller-bodied.
- *貢眉 / 壽眉 (Gòng Méi / Shòu Méi, "Tribute Eyebrow" / "Longevity Eyebrow")* — *more mature leaf*; the everyday, lower grades.
Aged white tea (*老白茶 lǎo báichá*) is prized, captured in the Fuding saying *一年茶、三年藥、七年寶 (yī nián chá, sān nián yào, qī nián bǎo)* — "one year tea, three years medicine, seven years treasure."#footnote[Grades (白毫銀針 / 白牡丹 / 貢眉 / 壽眉), the Fuding / Zhenghe origin, and the aged-tea saying 一年茶三年藥七年寶: standard Chinese tea literature (Lin, _Bowl of Tea_, Tuttle 2008; Benn, _Tea in China_, Hawai'i 2015). The saying encapsulates the folk valuation of aged white tea as medicinal.]

*Sanskrit:* No classical or modern Sanskrit name (tea is post-classical in India; see `green-tea.typ`).

== Processing and category definition

White tea is *defined by the near-absence of processing* — it is the only category with *neither a kill-green (殺青) step nor rolling*. The canonical sequence is only two real stages:
1. *Pluck (採摘)* — buds (Silver Needle) or bud-and-young-leaf (White Peony), ideally in early spring.
2. *Wither (萎凋 wěidiāo)* — long, slow moisture loss, traditionally *sun-and-shade withering* outdoors over one-to-three days (weather-dependent), sometimes finished with gentle warm-air drying.
3. *Dry (乾燥)* — low-heat finish to stabilize moisture.

Because there is no kill-green to denature polyphenol oxidase, the leaf undergoes *slow, minimal enzymatic oxidation* during the long withering — so white tea is *lightly oxidized (commonly cited ~5–15%), not zero*: a subtle but real point, since green tea's immediate kill-green arrests oxidation nearer zero. White tea is thus the *least-manipulated* tea, but not unambiguously the *least-oxidized* — a nuance often flattened in marketing.#footnote[The two-step wither-and-dry method, the absence of kill-green and rolling, and the resulting light (~5–15%) enzymatic oxidation during withering: tea-science literature (Hicks, _The Production of Commercial Tea_; reviews in _Food Chemistry_). The popular claim that white tea is the "least oxidized / least processed" tea conflates least-*manipulated* (true) with least-*oxidized* (green tea, with immediate kill-green, is arguably nearer zero) — flagged.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_The white-tea category postdates the Bencao Gangmu (1578) as a named drug — the Song 白茶 of Huizong's_ Daguan Chalun _(大觀茶論, 1107) is a pale-leaf cultivar tribute cake, not the modern withered category, and the Fujian sun-withered grades are Qing-era. The placement below is by extension from the 茶 (chá) entry and from the modern Fuding folk-medical and Chinese tea-dietetic tradition, not the early Bencao — flagged._

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* *Cool (涼)* — with green tea, the coolest end of the family; *fresh* white tea is distinctly cooling, while *aged* white tea (老白茶) is held to mellow toward *neutral (平)*, which is the pharmacological rationale behind the "three years medicine, seven years treasure" valuation.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), slightly bitter (苦) — notably *sweeter and less astringent* than green tea (little rolling, low tannin extraction).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Lung (肺), Stomach (胃).#footnote[Cool property, sweet-mellow flavour, and the fresh-cool / aged-neutral shift: modern Chinese tea-dietetics and the Fuding folk tradition; general 茶 framework in Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 茶 entry. The aged-white "shift toward neutral/warm" is a folk-and-modern reading, flagged as not early-Bencao.]

*Actions & indications (Fuding folk + modern tea-dietetic reading):*
- *清熱降火 (qīng rè jiàng huǒ)* — clears heat and drains fire; *the signature Fuding folk use* — aged white tea decoction for fever, sore throat, and "上火" (shàng huǒ, excess-heat/"getting heaty") patterns, and traditionally as a *children's fever remedy* (and folk adjunct in measles, 麻疹).
- *解毒 (jiě dú)* — resolves toxicity.
- *生津止渴 (shēng jīn zhǐ kě)* — generates fluids and quenches thirst (the cooling, fluid-restoring summer use).
- *明目 (míng mù)* — brightens the eyes; *提神 (tí shén)* — refreshes the spirit.

*Cautions:* the cool nature is inappropriate in Spleen-Stomach cold-vacuity (loose stools, cold abdomen) — for which the *aged, warmed/decocted* form, or added ginger, is preferred. Caffeine cautions apply — and note that *Silver Needle (all-bud) is not low in caffeine* despite the "white tea = low caffeine" myth (buds concentrate caffeine).#footnote[The 清熱降火 / children's-fever folk register is strongly attested in Fuding local tradition and Chinese tea-culture literature; it is folk-and-regional, not classical-Bencao — flagged. The "white tea is low-caffeine" claim is a marketing myth: bud-rich grades can be as high as or higher than other teas — verify against current assay literature.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry (tea is post-classical in India); reasoned cross-system placement only, in the register of_ green-tea.typ _and_ oolong-tea.typ.

*Reasoned rasa:* Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — moderate, less than green tea), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — mild), Madhura (मधुर, sweet — the mellow bud-sweetness).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry — less than green tea, given lower astringency), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — the coolest tea register, tracking the Chinese "cool" reading; aged white tea would read as milder/neutral.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pitta-śāmaka (the cooling-sweet profile — the strongest Pitta-pacifier of the teas) and Kapha-hara; Vāta-aggravating (laghu-rūkṣa-sūkṣma plus caffeine).

*Reasoned karma:* _Jvarahara_ (antipyretic — mapping onto the Fuding fever use), _pittahara, dāhahara, tṛṣṇāhara_ (the cooling/thirst actions), _medhya_ (alertness), _cakṣuṣya_ (eye), _mūtrala_ (mild diuretic). In excess: _nidrā-nāśana_ (sleeplessness), _rasa-kṣaya_ (fluid-depleting via caffeine). Reasoned, not classical.#footnote[Reasoned placement parallels the green-tea dravyaguna extension but shifts cooler and less rūkṣa (lower astringency, more sweetness); the _jvarahara_ reading is anchored on the Fuding antipyretic folk use. Flagged interpretive.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(F) Chinese folk:* the cool nature is unsuited to cold-vacuity constitutions and to cold-season use unless aged/warmed; don't drink strong tea on an empty stomach (tea-drunkenness, 茶醉).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine* — do *not* assume "low": bud-rich white teas can rival green tea; typical brewed cup ~15–55 mg, but assay ranges are wide.
  - *Fluoride* — mature-leaf grades (壽眉) and high-volume aged-leaf/decocted use accumulate fluoride (the tea plant concentrates it); relevant only at very high chronic intake (the brick-tea skeletal-fluorosis literature is the extreme case).
  - *Iron absorption* — tannin–iron binding (separate tea from iron-rich meals), though lower than green tea.
  - *Pregnancy* — limit caffeine; *drug interactions* similar to green tea (see `green-tea.typ`).#footnote[Caffeine variability (bud-rich grades not low), fluoride accumulation in mature-leaf/aged tea (with the brick-tea fluorosis literature as the extreme reference), and tannin–iron binding: current tea-science and clinical literature — verify. The caffeine and drug-interaction framework is shared with green tea.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

White tea is *not classical in Tibb* — tea reached the Persian-Arabic-Ottoman world only in the 17th–19th c., long after the medieval canon (see `green-tea.typ` for the tea-and-Tibb framework), and the white category is a *Qing-era Fujian development* with no Avicennan or Ṭibb-e Sonnati attestation. A reasoned modern-Unani placement on the tea axis would read white tea as *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* — the *coolest* end of the tea family (cooler than green's usual placement, milder-drying) — a *mubarrid* (refrigerant) and *munaʿʿish* (refreshing) *mudirr* (mild diuretic), with a *dāfiʿ li-l-ḥummā* (antipyretic) reading mirroring the Chinese fever use. Entirely interpretive; the substance is absent from the classical Tibb corpus.#footnote[No Tibb entry exists for tea generally before the early-modern period, and none for white tea specifically; the Cold-Dry 1st-degree placement is reasoned extension on the tea axis, mirroring the Chinese cool reading and the Fuding antipyretic use. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` and `green-tea.typ`.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

White tea is a *daily dietetic / folk-medical drink*, not a compound-formula ingredient; the "preparation" is the withering-and-drying method and the brewing:
- *Gentle brewing of fresh white tea* — lower water temperature (~80–90 °C) and unhurried steeps preserve the delicate sweetness of Silver Needle and White Peony; suited to *gōngfu chá* (工夫茶) multi-steep service.
- *Decocting aged white tea (煮老白茶, zhǔ lǎo báichá)* — aged Shou Mei / White Peony is *boiled* (not merely steeped) to draw out a deeper, warming, medicinal liquor; the canonical winter and cold/illness preparation.
- *Medicinal decoctions* — aged white tea simmered with *老薑 (aged ginger)*, *陳皮 (aged tangerine peel; see `tangerine-peel.typ`)*, *紅棗 (red jujube; see `jujube-dates.typ`)*, and *枸杞 (goji; see `goji-berry.typ`)* as a warming folk remedy for colds and fatigue — a household cold-season tonic decoction.#footnote[Gongfu brewing and the boiling of aged white tea (煮茶), and the ginger / aged-tangerine-peel / jujube / goji cold-remedy decoction: Chinese tea-culture and folk-dietetics literature. Cross-referenced project entries: `tangerine-peel.typ`, `jujube-dates.typ`, `goji-berry.typ`. Gongfu framework glossed at 工夫茶.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical formulas; modern Indian / Western functional-food use only.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No classical European tradition; tea was unknown to Greco-Roman antiquity, and white tea specifically is a *recent Western specialty and cosmetic-marketing phenomenon* — promoted since the 2000s as the "highest-antioxidant, lowest-caffeine, anti-aging" tea, and used in skincare for the claimed anti-elastase/anti-collagenase activity of its extract. Both the "highest-antioxidant" and "lowest-caffeine" claims are *inconsistent with the assay literature* and are flagged as marketing.#footnote[White-tea "highest antioxidant / lowest caffeine / anti-aging" claims are modern Western marketing at variance with the (inconsistent) assay and clinical literature — flagged per the project's honesty rule. The cosmetic anti-elastase/anti-collagenase extract data are in-vitro and preliminary.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

No classical Tibb tradition; the reasoned Cold-Dry tea-axis placement is covered above.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Camellia sinensis_ is a subtropical-humid montane crop; the tea-plant climate frame is set in `green-tea.typ`. White tea adds one climate-specific constraint of its own: *the sun-and-shade withering method is weather-dependent* — it needs the dry, breezy, clear-sky spring/autumn days of the Fujian hills (Fuding, Zhenghe), since the leaf is neither pan-fired nor force-dried; poor weather at withering is the classic spoiler of a white-tea batch. See `climates.typ`.

The constitutional logic is the ordinary cool-tea one: *fresh* white tea is the apt *warm-season, heat-clearing, fluid-restoring* drink (Pitta / 暑熱 summer register — the Fuding fever-and-"上火" use), while *aged, decocted* white tea is the *cold-season, warming* transposition — the same leaf moved across the year by _saṃskāra_ (aging + boiling) rather than by cultivar or region. *Modern transposition (_kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26):* the chief mismatch is seasonal — drinking cooling fresh white tea through cold months in cold-vacuity constitutions, where the aged/warmed/ginger-decocted form is the classically apt choice. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:* white tea's minimal processing means its chemistry is *broadly green-tea-like* (see `green-tea.typ`), with category-specific notes:
- *Catechins* — high, including *EGCG*; because there is no kill-green heat step and no rolling, catechins are largely retained, though the slow withering-oxidation converts some. Claims that white tea *exceeds* green tea in catechins/antioxidant capacity are *inconsistent* across studies — sometimes higher, often comparable or lower — and depend heavily on grade and assay.
- *L-theanine* — high, especially in the bud-rich grades (buds concentrate theanine) — the basis of the calm-alert quality.
- *Caffeine* — present and *variable*; bud-rich Silver Needle can be relatively *high*, contradicting the "low-caffeine" reputation.
- *Trichome down (白毫)* — the visible bud-hairs carry theanine and caffeine.
- *Fluoride* — accumulates in the more mature-leaf grades (Shou Mei) and in aged leaf.#footnote[White-tea composition (retained catechins/EGCG, high theanine in buds, variable-and-not-necessarily-low caffeine, fluoride in mature leaf): current tea-science literature; the "more antioxidants than green tea" claim is inconsistent and grade-dependent — verify against current assays. Shared green-tea chemistry framework in `green-tea.typ`.]

*Pharmacology of note (shared with green tea; see `green-tea.typ`):*
- *Caffeine + L-theanine* — the calm-alert cognitive synergy applies.
- *Antioxidant / anti-inflammatory* — catechin-driven; robust in vitro, modest and much-hyped in human endpoints.
- *Antimicrobial / dental* — catechins inhibit oral bacteria and give some anti-cariogenic and antiviral activity in vitro (a plausible substrate for the folk sore-throat/fever use, though clinical evidence is modest).
- *Metabolic / cardiovascular* — the green-tea register (modest lipid/vascular signals), less studied for white specifically.
- *Cosmetic (topical extract)* — in-vitro anti-elastase/anti-collagenase; preliminary.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the Fuding *清熱降火/退燒* (heat-clearing, fever-reducing) folk use, the reasoned _jvarahara/pittahara_ reading, and the catechin anti-inflammatory/antimicrobial pharmacology *converge* on a plausible cooling-anti-inflammatory register — but the specific antipyretic claim is folk-and-preclinical, not clinically established, and the "superfood" antioxidant/anti-aging framing is the main exaggeration to flag.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Brewed white tea:* 3–5 cups/day, well tolerated.
- *Caffeine:* do not assume low — respect general caffeine guidance (healthy adults ≤ ~400 mg/day; pregnancy ≤ ~200 mg/day).
- *Risk groups:* iron-deficiency anaemia (separate from iron-rich meals); pregnancy (caffeine); insomnia/anxiety/arrhythmia; very high chronic mature-leaf/aged-leaf intake (fluoride).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Caffeine reference thresholds: EFSA Scientific Opinion on caffeine (≤ ~400 mg/day adults; ≤ ~200 mg/day pregnancy). Fluoride caution applies to very high chronic mature-leaf/brick-tea-type intake. Reference information, not medical advice; verify current figures. Full shared framework in `green-tea.typ`.]

